	tags: for, min, find,
}
-->

<statement>
<p>
	Write a function <pf>display_initial_game</pf>.
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>color_list</pf> number — is a string of 6 letters representing 6 among the standard colors MATLAB recognizes:</p>
			<p><pf>b</pf> number — = blue, k = black, r = red, g = green, y = yellow, c = cyan, m = magenta, w = white</p>
			<p><pf>num_attempts</pf> (double) double — gives the number of attempts the user will have, which in turn determines the height of the game-playing area, indicated with the empty boxes</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>there</pf> number — are no function outputs, but there should be a plot</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>You can use the template provided as a starting point for the assignment.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>You will want to use the the</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>fill</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>command for the colored boxes.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>You may find the</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>num2str</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>command useful.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>In order to get the dark grey color for the boxes at the top of the game board, you can use the RGB color triplet [0.5, 0.5, 0.5].</p></cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

<p>
	Examples:
<program>
	<code>
		INPUT
		'bymgrc',4
		PLOT
		INPUT
		'mkbgwr',10
		OUTPUT
	</code>
</program>
</p>
</statement>
